  "step_3": "The decision reason requires comparing the probabilities of loss and preferring the lottery with the lower probability of loss. As established, Lottery B has a 0% chance of loss (if 5 Euros is the reference point), while Lottery A has a 99.9% chance of loss, so the reason would favor B. However, the individual chooses A and justifies this by the absence of a negative outcome (i.e., no financial loss) and the high upside potential. The individual does not consider the relative likelihood of experiencing a loss compared to B; instead, they note that A does not result in a loss in absolute terms (i.e., no debt or negative payoff). This reflects a different reasoning pattern—possibly loss aversion with a zero reference point or a focus on maximum gain—rather than a comparison of loss probabilities. The information considered (presence of high gain, no negative outcome) and the justification (no loss in worst case) do not align with the decision reason’s focus on minimizing the probability of loss across lotteries. Therefore, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
}

